# {{ .Project }} Simple API

Rails-style classic CRUD profile using EF Core and direct Minimal API endpoints. Entities are simple Active Record-like models backed by `AppDbContext`.

## Build and run

Run from the generated project root:

```powershell
dotnet restore .\{{ .Project }}.sln
dotnet build .\{{ .Project }}.sln
dotnet run --project .\src\WebApi\WebApi.csproj --urls http://localhost:5000
```

Check the API at `http://localhost:5000/health`. The default database is SQLite and is stored as `{{ kebab .Project }}.db` in the Web API working directory.
{{- if eq .Arch "ar" }}

## CI / production build

`.\scripts\ci.ps1` runs restore, build, and test in one step, and can publish
the WebApi host with `-Publish` (skip tests with `-SkipTests`, override
configuration with `-Configuration Debug`):

```powershell
.\scripts\ci.ps1 -Publish
```
{{- end }}

Add a CRUD entity after the project exists:

```powershell
aspgen add entity Book title:string author:string pages:int published:date available:bool --project .
```

The generated WPF client uses the API when the project is `fullstack`. Set `ASPGENT_API_URL` before starting the desktop app to override the default `http://localhost:5000`.

Example:

```text
$env:ASPGENT_API_URL = "https://localhost:7001"
dotnet run --project src/Desktop/{{ .Project }}.Desktop.csproj
```
